            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>Problem Background</h2>
<p>AI-generated ad copy is fast, but when you check the results, similarity with competitors reaches 70%. CTR drops, costs rise, and the team falls into the trap: more AI output means less differentiation.</p>
<p>The root cause: most users treat AI as a "copy machine" — just feed it a product name and let it go. In reality, AI output quality depends entirely on differentiated instructions you provide.</p>
<p>This article provides a practical SOP to systematically avoid AI copy homogenization.</p>
<hr />
<h2>SOP Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Define Brand Differentiation Positioning</h3>
<p><strong>What to do:</strong> Before using AI, answer three questions internally:</p>
<ol>
<li>Who is our target user? (Be specific)</li>
<li>What is our core competitive advantage? (Not features — the reason users choose you)</li>
<li>What copy patterns do competitors commonly use? (List 3-5 high-frequency words or phrases as restrictions)</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Deliverable:</strong> A "Brand Copy Differentiation Guide" including: user pain point keywords, competitor common words to avoid, brand tone guidelines.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep it concise — the key is ensuring everyone involved in copy production knows what must be said and what must not.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Build a "Copy Restriction List"</h3>
<p><strong>What to do:</strong> Collect high-exposure competitor creatives from the past 30 days, extract recurring phrases and words, and add them to a blacklist.</p>
<p><strong>How to do it:</strong><br />
- Open Meta Ad Library or third-party tools<br />
- Filter same-category, high-frequency creatives<br />
- Note high-frequency words like: "Limited time offer," "Don't miss out," "#1 in sales"<br />
- Mark these as "prohibited" and explicitly exclude them in AI prompts</p>
<p><strong>Common restriction categories:</strong><br />
- Absolute language (100%, #1, best)<br />
- Overpromising (money-back guarantee, instant results)<br />
- Homogenized openings (Do you know? Just 3 steps)</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Design Differentiated AI Prompts</h3>
<p><strong>What to do:</strong> Set clear differentiated writing frameworks for AI instead of letting it freestyle.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended prompt structure:</strong></p>
<pre><code>【Product】+ 【Target User】+ 【Core Benefit】+ 【Differentiation Angle】+ 【Restricted Words】+ 【Length/Format】
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Example comparison:</strong></p>
<p>❌ Ineffective prompt:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Write an ad copy for an electric toothbrush</p>
</blockquote>
<p>✅ Effective prompt:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Write an electric toothbrush ad copy targeting 25-35 year old urban women, highlighting "quiet and low-noise so it won't disturb family," using first-person vlog style, avoiding words like "limited offer," "great value," "#1," within 50 characters.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Key techniques:</strong><br />
- Give AI a specific "persona" — e.g., "You are a career woman who values life rituals"<br />
- Provide a differentiation angle — let AI approach from a specific perspective<br />
- Specify output format — reduce AI's room for improvisation</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 4: A/B Test to Verify Differentiation Effectiveness</h3>
<p><strong>What to do:</strong> After generating copy, verify differentiation effectiveness through small-budget testing.</p>
<p><strong>Testing matrix:</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Dimension</th>
<th>Example</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Opening style</td>
<td>Question vs statement vs scene description</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Benefit presentation</td>
<td>Feature-focused vs user pain point-focused</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Tone style</td>
<td>Professional vs friendly vs humorous</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Success criteria:</strong><br />
- CTR 20%+ above category average<br />
- CPC 15%+ below category average<br />
- Conversion rate moves in same direction as CTR</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> Keep audience and placement consistent during testing — only change copy variables.</p>
<hr />
<h3>Step 5: Regular Review and Iteration</h3>
<p><strong>What to do:</strong> Set a fixed weekly time to review copy performance, eliminate low-performing creatives, and update prompt library.</p>
<p><strong>Review checklist:</strong><br />
- What are the 3 best-performing copies this week? What do they have in common?<br />
- What are the 3 worst-performing copies? Which restrictions did they violate?<br />
- Have competitors introduced new copy patterns recently?<br />
- Does the prompt template need adjustment?</p>
<p><strong>Continuous optimization actions:</strong><br />
- Document effective copy structures as new templates<br />
- Add violated words to the restriction list<br />
- Update the "Brand Copy Differentiation Guide" regularly</p>
<hr />
<h2>Execution Checklist</h2>
<p>Before starting AI-powered ad copy production, confirm each item:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Brand differentiation positioning has been defined and documented</li>
<li>[ ] Competitor high-frequency word restriction list has been established</li>
<li>[ ] AI prompt template with differentiation angle has been designed</li>
<li>[ ] A/B testing plan has been planned (start with small budget)</li>
<li>[ ] Weekly review time slots have been scheduled</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Only proceed to batch production after all items are checked.</strong></p>
<hr />
<h2>Key Takeaway</h2>
<p>AI is a tool, not the answer itself. Homogenization stems not from AI's limited capability but from unsystematic usage. By following this 5-step SOP—positioning → restriction building → prompt design → testing → iteration—teams can systematically improve AI copy differentiation, reduce creative overlap risk, and boost overall ad ROI.</p>
<p><strong>Core principle:</strong> Let AI perform within your framework, not follow the market's crowd.</p>
